---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Feature Request from the Boss - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="feature-request">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="The agent can turn a vague, rushed request into something buildable: work out what's being asked, ask or decide what it leaves out, build it, and draft the reply.">
			The Head of Claims Operations needs something for tomorrow's board meeting. All you have is a short email from a phone and a
			screenshot with red circles on it.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message
			kind="email"
			subject="URGENT export"
			from="G. Whitlock, Head of Claims Operations"
			to="Web Team"
			date="Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:42"
			attachments={[{ name: "export-request.jpg", href: "/downloads/kaiju-mutual/15.export-request.jpg" }]}
		>
			<p>
				Board meeting tomorrow 9am. I need all of this in Excel so I can do my pivot tables. Should be easy, it's just a button.
				Also make it show the important ones.
			</p>
			<p>G</p>
			<p class="text-stone-400">Sent from my iPhone</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent work out what Whitlock is asking for, build it, and draft a reply.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Work out which page the screenshot shows and where the button goes.",
				"Ask about, or decide and explain, what the email leaves out.",
				"Add an export to that page that opens in Excel with the right columns.",
				"Make sure Excel can sum the amounts and read the dates.",
				"Say what it took \"the important ones\" to mean, and why.",
				"Draft a short reply to Whitlock: what was built, what was decided for them, and how to use it.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`My boss sent this email and screenshot: <email> <image>. Work out which page it is and what they're asking for.

Before you build anything, list what the request leaves out and either ask me or pick a sensible answer and say why. It has to open in Excel for pivot tables, so check that numbers and dates come through as values Excel can use.

Then build it, and draft a short reply to them without jargon: what you built, what you decided for them, and how to use it.`}
		>
			<p>What the request leaves out, and what a good agent asks about or decides and says so:</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
				<li>
					<strong>"Excel":</strong> CSV opens in Excel with no library. An agent that adds an <code>.xlsx</code> library is
					over-building.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>"All of this":</strong> the claims queue is paged and filtered by stage tab. For pivot tables, all rows in the
					current tab is the likely answer, not just the 25 on screen.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>"The important ones":</strong> the screenshot circles a $3,400,000 estimate. That could mean sort, filter or
					highlight, and nobody gave a threshold. Sorting by estimate, highest first, and saying so in the reply is reasonable.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What Excel cares about:</strong> estimates as plain numbers, not "$3,400,000" strings, dates Excel can read,
					commas and quotes in claim titles escaped, and a UTF-8 BOM so accented names open correctly.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The reply is part of the work. It should mention every decision taken on Whitlock's behalf, so they can push back before
				the board meeting, not during it.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
